**Read:** [Manufacturing — The Open University](../sources/Manufacturing-Open-University.lingua#r_5WVG0HEJ30KYA0GMVCHEZBY9X0), cutting and surface engineering; the shared mechanical surface-finishing Record. Return to fracture, fatigue and thermal gradients.

Machining and grinding remove material while imposing heat, plastic strain and contact. The final surface can differ from the bulk through roughness, residual stress, transformation, microcracks, smeared phases or contamination. A dimensional inspection alone does not establish surface integrity; a low roughness number does not establish a beneficial residual-stress state.

**Work:** Compare two finishing histories for one alloy or ceramic, documenting tool, fluid, force and thermal evidence. Identify which layer a hardness, diffraction or microscopy measurement samples. Relate that state to the relevant service loading rather than assuming every harder surface is better. Follow the shared grinding account for machine/process details and retain the material-state evidence in the production history.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
